In this paper HD 189733b and its Hot Jupiter was examined for some time ranging from before its transit to after its  secondary eclipse. When examnining the large number of pixels sysetmatic error was found from a detector induced rise in up to 10% of pixels over time of its illumination value. High illumination pixels were found to converge to a constant value by the time the star and planet were transiting each other, and lower illumination pixels followed a linear increase over time. To fix this systematic error a median illumination was estimated and applied to lower pixel value levels, about 33% of total flux is contributed by corrected pixels and the amplitude reduced by only 13% of total size. The flux increase at two spots on the star may come from it being an active star with spots causing this flux variation. There was a delay found for the start of the secondary eclipse and it is speculated on its origin. Ultimately it is ruled to be from an slightly eccentric orbit that was too small to be dected by radial velocity measurements. Atmospheric contrast models are made for the day vs night side temperatures, planetary response to stellar irradiation coming from comparisons from the radiative time scale and advection time scale. The model suggests the two time scales are comparable and result in temperature differences of up to 1000K. The flux is assumed to come from deeper in the atmosphere where possible supersonic winds speeds are assumed. \
