In this article, Spitzer Space Telescope IR data of the HD 189733 exoplanet system is evaluted at multiple flux ratios at wavelenghts of 3.6, 4.5, 5.8, 8.0, and 24 microns. Previous data is reanalyzed to retrive a band at 16 microns as well. Transmission spectroscopy is used with IR measurements to get better measurments of the planet to star contrast ratio that helps constrain atmospheric quantites like chemistry and temperature on its differing day and night sides. There was lots of filteration of the data to disregard some pixels entirely, fitting the data to a quadratifc function of ln change in t. The pixels individually increase over time and has a detector ramp effect, the strongly illuminated pixels coverge to a constant while the weaker illuminated pixels increase linearly over time. The system was observed for 5 hours for the secondary eclipse, the pixel sensitivity was accounted for by normallizing each time series to a common scale. Pixels larger than 3 simga from the median are trimmed off, background is subtracted individually. Comparing band models, it is seen at longer wavelengths that a hole appears due to water absorption, but in shorter wavelenghts the data suggests water is in emission. The two planets are compared, viewing their atmospheric data and temperature inversion qualities, as well as comparing their physical size and densitiy. The longer wavelenghts at 16 and 24 microns suggest a small day night variation on HD 189733b, eclipse depth at 8 microns also shows dayside emission may vary in time, allowing for weather to be studied on the planet.\
